# ruff: noqa: N999  (module is named after the "in" background code the builder imports by name)
"""Indian-name passages (Hindi / Tamil / Bengali romanisation, mythic and contemporary). Original prose."""

PASSAGES = [
("in01", """[[Arjun|p]] waited under the banyan tree outside [[Hastinapur Fort|l]]. “[[Arjun-ji|p|tit]],” said the gatekeeper, “the [[maharaj|t]] will see you now.” The old king had two sons, and each was called [[Yuvraj|t|sh]] by his own faction. “Which one?” asked [[Arjun|p]]. The guard shrugged. Hope was a luxury in that court. Far off, a conch sounded from the [[Sun Temple|l]]. [[Guruji|p|tit]] had warned him: speak little, bow low, and keep the bow named [[Moon Arrow|i]] hidden beneath your cloak."""),
("in02", """[[Priya Sharma|p]] dropped her bag in the hall. “[[Sharma-ji|p|tit]]'s daughter is back,” the neighbours whispered, and “[[Priya-didi|p|tit]]!” cried the children. To her father she was only [[Beti|p|ali]]. In [[Lajpat Nagar|l]], everyone knew the family on the corner, and there were three households of that surname on the same street, none related. “Which [[Sharma-ji|p|tit]]?” someone always asked. The tea at the corner stall, [[Chai Wala Raju|p|tit]]'s famous brew, steamed beside the door of the [[Lotus Cooperative Bank|o]]."""),
("in03", """The village headman and the temple headman of [[Kanchipuram|l]] shared the title [[Periyavar|t|sh]], which means elder. When [[Murugan|p]] arrived, he was unsure whom to greet. “[[Periyavar|t|sh]] is at the river,” a boy said. “Which?” “The tall one.” [[Murugan|p]] touched his forehead. “[[Thambi|p|tit]],” called an old woman, “bring water.” Beyond the paddy fields rose the gopuram of the [[Shiva Temple|l]], where the bronze lamp [[Eternal Flame|i]] had burned since before the [[Pallava Dynasty|o]] fell."""),
("in04", """[[Joy|p|si]] was the nickname of [[Joydeep Ghosh|p]], and in the lanes of [[Kolkata|l]] he was [[Joy-da|p|tit]] to every child. “[[Joy-da|p|tit]], the cinema!” “Not today.” His friend [[Mitra|p]] called him [[Joydeep-babu|p|tit]] to tease him. Rain had turned the road to mud. At the [[Bengal Film Society|o]] they showed old reels, and the projectionist, [[Mr. Dutta|p|tit]], knew every frame. “[[Dutta-babu|p|tit]],” whispered [[Joy|p|wl]], “do you have the lost reel?” The old man smiled and tapped a tin marked [[Midnight Raga|i]]."""),
("in05", """Grace is a word of English origin, but [[Grace Menon|p|wl]] was born in Kerala. At the [[Holy Cross School|o]] she was [[Grace-miss|p|tit]], and at home she was [[Gracy|p]]. “[[Menon-madam|p|tit]], your result is out,” the clerk said. Nobody told her the principal, the [[Sister Superior|t]], had already read it. In the corridor, [[Sister Agnes|p|tit]] folded a paper boat. Monsoon wind pushed at the shutters of [[Kottayam|l]]. “Do not worry, [[Grace|p|wl]],” she said. “God has plans.”"""),
("in06", """[[Raj|p]] and [[Raja|p]] were brothers, and the second was the elder, which confused everyone. In the market of [[Jaipur|l]], traders called [[Raja-bhai|p|tit]] the clever one. “[[Raj|p]] will inherit the shop,” said their mother. “Not if the [[Marwari Merchants' Guild|o]] has anything to say.” The head of the guild, a stern man known only as [[Seth-ji|p|tit]], had come to collect. Beside the ledger lay the old brass lamp, [[Aladdin's Cousin|i]], that the family refused to sell."""),
("in07", """“[[Hunter|p|si]],” said [[Rani Devi|p]], “is what the British called him.” [[Hunter|p|wl]] was in fact a Gurkha scout named [[Bahadur Thapa|p]]. The [[queen|t]] of [[Jhansi|l]] used a third name for him, [[Chhoti Tope|p|ali]]. In the court of [[Rani Devi|p]] each name meant one man. Dust blew along the walls of the [[Red Fort of Jhansi|l]]. “[[Bahadur-ji|p|tit]],” said the [[queen|t]], “have the cannons ready.” He bowed. The silver sword named [[Lakshmi's Edge|i]] hung at her hip."""),
("in08", """The [[Holi Festival Committee|o]] met at [[Krishna Apartments|l]]. [[Mr. Verma|p|tit]], the secretary, and another [[Mr. Verma|p|tit]], the treasurer, glared at each other across the table. “[[Verma-sahab|p|tit]],” said the [[chairman|t]], “please decide which of you will speak.” Both rose. The [[chairman|t]] sighed and handed [[Tara|p]] the gavel. [[Tara|p|si]], who was twelve and the only sensible person present, banged it. “Colours at five,” she said, and the committee obeyed without a word."""),
("in09", """[[Hope Nair|p|si]] ran a clinic in [[Mysuru|l]]. [[Hope|p|wl]] was her real first name, chosen by a missionary grandmother. Locals said [[Dr. Hope|p|tit]] or [[Doctor-amma|p|tit]]. “[[Amma|p|tit]],” called a patient's child, “mummy is sick.” The clinic, called [[House of Mercy|o]], had four beds and one fan. The district [[collector|t]] visited once a year, and promised a grant each time. “[[Hope Nair|p|wl]],” he said now, “your clinic will be funded.” She had heard it before, and kept her notebook, [[The Green Ledger|i]], in her bag."""),
("in10", """[[Vikram|p]] sat in the audience of the [[Mumbai Sabha|o]] with his hand raised. “[[Vikram-ji|p|tit]], your question?” “Respected [[Chairperson|t]], my question is about water.” The [[chairperson|t]] was [[Smt. Kapoor|p|tit]], known in the press as [[the Iron Lady of Dadar|t|ali]]. Laughter rippled. The hall at [[Shivaji Park|l]] was packed. “[[Vikram bhai|p|tit]],” someone said, “sit down.” “Water first,” said [[Vikram|p]]. It was the oldest argument in the city."""),
("in11", """Long ago, in the kingdom of [[Kalinga|l]], two brothers shared the title [[Raja|t|sh]]. The elder ruled the hills, the younger the plains. Both were called [[Raja|t|sh]] by their people, and [[Ashoka|p]] was the only one who ever dared to ask which. [[Prince Ashoka|p|tit]] was neither, and was the king's nephew. His horse, [[Moonrise|i]], had no rival. At the gate of [[Dhauli|l]], the old [[Mahamatya|t]] bowed and said, “[[Yuvraj|t]], the war is lost.” Nobody argued."""),
("in12", """[[Lakshmi|p]] walked into the kitchen and found her grandmother already awake. “[[Ammamma|p|tit]],” she said, “the [[Bank of Madras|o]] sent a letter.” The old woman did not look up. “Read it, [[Lakshmi-kutty|p|tit]].” The letter said that the family house in [[Mylapore|l]], called [[Anandam|l]], would be sold at auction. [[Ammamma|p|tit]] folded her hands. Sentimental objects, like the brass vessel [[Kamadhenu|i]], cannot be put on a ledger. “Then we will see,” she said."""),
("in13", """Two priests in [[Varanasi|l]] answered to [[Panditji|t|sh]]. The first, [[Pandit Shastri|p|tit]], taught at the ghat. The second, [[Pandit Joshi|p|tit]], kept the temple of [[Kashi Vishwanath|l]]. A pilgrim asked, “[[Panditji|t|sh]], where may I pray?” “Which?” they both said. The pilgrim, [[Rohan|p]], bowed in confusion. Smoke rose from the ghat, and a boatman called [[Kallu|p]] laughed at them all. Near the boat lay a conch known as the [[Voice of Ganga|i]], silent."""),
("in14", """[[Sunita|p]] and [[Sunil|p]] opened the shop on [[Linking Road|l]]. People said [[Sunita-ji|p|tit]] was the shrewder. “Ji,” said a customer, “which saree is best?” “Ask [[Sunil|p]].” The business was called [[Sun & Moon Silks|o]], though neither of them wanted the moon. The landlord, a quiet Parsi called [[Mr. Dastur|p|tit]], collected the rent. On the shelf, between rolls of cloth, lay a carved box named [[Peacock's Secret|i]]. Nobody had ever opened it."""),
("in15", """“[[Kabir|p]]!” called [[Maa|p|tit]] from the courtyard. “Eat!” The family compound in [[Lucknow|l]] held three generations, and each had a different name for the same boy: [[Kabir|p]] to his mother, [[Kabir Khan|p]] on paper, [[Chhotu|p|ali]] to the cousins. At the [[Imambara Trust|o]], an old clerk stamped his admission form. “[[Khan-sahab|p|tit]],” the clerk said, “welcome.” Outside, pigeons rose from the roof of the [[Bara Imambara|l]]. [[Kabir|p]] ran toward the gate, carrying a worn toy named [[Pagal Ghoda|i]]."""),
]
